output apimServiceName string = apimService.name
output apimGatewayUrl string = apimService.properties.gatewayUrl
// The public IPs of this gateway instance, consumed by every backend web app as
// its inbound allow rule. This is the VIP the gateway both receives on and calls
// out from when it is not injected into a virtual network, which is how these
// backends see it. Exposed as the whole array rather than a single element so a
// multi-unit gateway is admitted in full.
//
// Emitted ONLY for tiers that have a dedicated IP. On a shared-infrastructure
// tier the property does not exist, and an output referencing it fails the
// deployment at output-evaluation time (DeploymentOutputEvaluationFailed:
// "Expected a value of type 'Array', but received a value of type 'Null'") --
// after every resource has already been created.
//
// NOT outboundPublicIPAddresses: per the ARM schema that property carries NAT
// Gateway prefixes and is populated only for Premium SKU on stv2, so it is
// empty for the SKU this template provisions by default.
output apimPublicIpAddresses array = apimService.properties.publicIPAddresses
// Source prefixes that admit this gateway, in the form a network security rule
// takes verbatim. One output rather than two so a backend's admit rule never
// has to know which tier fronts it -- the tier-dependent part is resolved here,
// where the tier is chosen.
// Dedicated tier: the instance's own IPs, each as a single-address prefix, so
// the rule admits this gateway and nothing else.
//
// map(), NOT a for-expression. A for-expression compiles to an ARM copy loop,
// whose count must be resolvable at the START of the deployment -- and this
// instance's IP list is assigned when the gateway is created, so the template
// fails to compile with BCP178 ("This expression is being used in the
// for-expression, which requires a value that can be calculated at the start of
// the deployment"), taking main.bicep down with BCP104 before a single resource
// is touched. map() is a plain ARM function over the same array: outputs are
// evaluated after every resource exists, so the reference resolves there. The
// documented lambda restriction is on a lambda VARIABLE appearing inside the
// reference / list-key resource functions, which is not what this does -- the
// resource reference is map's input, and the lambda body only formats the
// element it is handed.
output apimEdgeSourceAddressPrefixes array = map(apimService.properties.publicIPAddresses, apimIp => '${apimIp}/32')
// The system-assigned (trusted-caller) identity's principal -- the only
// identity whose principal any consumer needs. The certificate-reader
// identity's grant is made by apim-tls-certificate-vault.bicep.
output apimPrincipalId string = apimService.identity.principalId
